WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.902
-Our first guest is
an Oscar-nominated actor

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:05.938
who stars in the new movie,
"Mainstream," which is available

00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:08.474
digitally and in select theaters
this Friday.

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:10.743
Please welcome Andrew Garfield!

00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:11.844
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:13.879
-Oh, that's what
I'm talking about!

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:16.315
Aaah!
Oh, my goodness!

00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:19.885
Ah, I miss you, buddy.
-I miss you!

00:00:19.885 --> 00:00:21.520
I love you!
I'm hugging you.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.256
Tariq is
the greatest rapper alive.

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:25.792
Happy Teacher Appreciation Day.
-I mean --

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:28.061
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:29.595
You summed it all up
right there.

00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:31.730
We have
all amazing teachers here.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.566
While we're speaking of that,
do you have a teacher

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.501
you'd like to give
a shout-out to?

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:38.137
-Yes, Mr. Tong.
Mr. Philip Tong.

00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:41.174
I would not be anywhere
without his brilliant

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:44.878
drama teaching when I needed
guidance in my life,

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:48.347
'cause my previous drama teacher
had thrown a chair at my head,

00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:52.485
and that was the extent
of my relationship with him.

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:54.320
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.188
-Wow!
That's -- That's crazy.

00:00:56.188 --> 00:00:57.890
In drama --
So that's a lot of drama.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:58.958
[ Laughter ]
-That's a lot of --

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:01.127
I brought the drama.
-You really did.

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:04.764
-[ Laughs ]
No, like, so Mr. Tong,

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:07.666
he came into my life
when I was really kind of lost

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:10.169
as a 15-, 16-year-old.

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:11.904
Didn't really know what
I wanted to do with my life,

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:14.707
and I had kind of given up
on everything.

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:16.108
It was kind of like
"The Truman Show,"

00:01:16.108 --> 00:01:18.178
where you feel like there's
something else out there for you

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:20.813
but you don't -- you haven't got
the courage to get on the boat

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:23.750
and go through the kind of
cardboard sky

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.484
at the back of the lot.

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:29.655
And Mr. Tong came around,
and he arrived at our school,

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:32.057
and he saw me do a school play,

00:01:32.057 --> 00:01:33.926
and it was like
a moment of mentorship,

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:35.661
which we all need
at some point in our lives

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:39.599
to put us on our path, our
kind of deepest path, I think.

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:43.502
And he had the generosity
and the foolishness

00:01:43.502 --> 00:01:45.371
to come up to me and say,
"Hey, I think you have talent

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:48.174
in this, and I'd like you
to study with me next year,"

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:49.575
and then he introduced me
to theater,

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:51.644
and he introduced me
to the arts.

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.913
And it was like
I finally was Jim Carrey

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:56.616
walking through that door
in the sky.

00:01:56.616 --> 00:02:00.620
And I entered my life.
And so, Mr. Philip Tong,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.523
in Surrey, you changed my life
and to all the teachers

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:06.725
who are devoted teachers,
oh, I just bow.

00:02:06.725 --> 00:02:08.895
I bow deeply
out of camera to you.

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:14.033
-I love that.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:16.802
Obviously, this past year,
everyone was just like,

00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:20.873
"Oh, my gosh, what teachers do,
it's just above and beyond."

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:22.975
You go like, "Wait.
We have to just appreciate them

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:25.244
every single day."

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:27.747
But also the front-line workers,
as well,

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:32.051
and people that are out there,
just making everything happen.

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:33.719
I know your brother,

00:02:33.719 --> 00:02:36.889
you'd like to give a shout-out
to, who's a doctor.

00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:38.691
-Yeah, Ben.
Benjamin Garfield.

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:41.627
He'll be very upset
that I'm mentioning his name.

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:44.864
-That's what brothers are for.
-Yeah, that's true.

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:49.068
But he's also, you know, he kind
of exemplifies what it is to be,

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:51.037
you know,
to be of service to people.

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:54.273
He's a lung doctor.
He's a pulmonary specialist,

00:02:54.273 --> 00:02:56.976
so he's been on the front lines
of this and, you know,

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.178
moving ventilators,
deciding where vent--

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:02.148
It's just been --
you know, been the most intense

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:03.683
thing in the world for him.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.351
But he's the guy
that you want there.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:08.454
He's the guy, like most
front-line workers that I know,

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:11.590
they're the people that spend,
you know, three hours too long

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:15.828
at work because they're just
so devoted and, you know,

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:19.265
I've seen him in such
a new light this year.

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:21.701
I always knew he was heroic,
but he doesn't try to be heroic,

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:23.169
and that's the difference.
He just --

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:24.470
He's called to what he does.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.705
He just wants to help people
and heal people.

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:29.608
So I love you, Ben,
and everyone that you work with

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:30.877
at the Brompton Hospital.

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:33.246
Thank you for everything
you've done this year.

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:35.548
-Ben Garfield.
[ Applause ]

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:37.583
Thank you, Ben.
-He'll get a kick.

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.785
He'll get a kick out of you
mentioning his name, man.

00:03:39.785 --> 00:03:41.921
That's awesome.
That's awesome.

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.956
-I know you just wrapped a movie

00:03:43.956 --> 00:03:46.192
with our friend,
Lin-Manuel Miranda.

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:48.794
-Yes.
-"tick, tick...Boom!"

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:51.864
And Lin,
this is his directorial debut.

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:53.900
-Yeah.
-How was he?

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.768
Was he nervous?
Was he excited?

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.071
Was he --
-Dude, you know Lin.

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:02.742
He's like this crazy mixture of
the most precocious 8-year-old,

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:06.045
like, genius student of life...
-Yeah.

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:08.547
-...like, that won't stop
talking and has a reference

00:04:08.547 --> 00:04:12.117
for everything, and,
simultaneously, he's maybe

00:04:12.117 --> 00:04:14.486
one of the ultimate
creative geniuses of our time.

00:04:14.486 --> 00:04:18.090
It's this weird combination
of having, like,

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.026
the most precocious
8-year-old around,

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:22.462
like, running around
your ankles,

00:04:22.462 --> 00:04:25.698
while also
having written "Hamilton."

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:27.333
-Yeah, exactly!
The guy's a genius.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.534
-But like --
-But he also has, like...

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:30.803
-Yeah, like every moment.
-...deep cuts, and, like,

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:32.205
he'll make a reference.

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:34.340
It will be like, "Wait.
Did you just say that?"

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.375
-Yeah.
Like, at the Oscars,

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:37.944
when he made a reference to the

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:40.046
"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles"
theme song.

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:43.682
And I was like, "No one's going
to understand this, maybe."

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:44.817
So I text, it was like, "Whoa!

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:47.453
You just referenced
'TMNT' theme song!"

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:50.356
And he was like, "I know who the
true nerds are in my phone book

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:51.757
because it was you
and one other person

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:54.093
that just texted me this."

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:55.728
No, I love him, man.
I love him.

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:58.698
Like, to get to work with him
on this was just --

00:04:58.698 --> 00:05:00.233
Oh, man, it was a privilege.

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:02.267
And I got to work with Tariq
for a day or two.

00:05:02.267 --> 00:05:05.003
I don't know if I'm supposed to
say that, but I just said it.

00:05:05.003 --> 00:05:07.540
And I got to witness
the genius in the flesh.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.909
-Yeah, he's pretty amazing.

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:13.913
Well, I want to talk about
your new film, "Mainstream."

00:05:13.913 --> 00:05:18.150
This is a very interesting idea
for a film, and this is from --

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.454
Is it Gia Coppola?
-Yeah, Gia Coppola.

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:24.724
She's made one movie previously,
called "Palo Alto,"

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:26.458
which you should check out.
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:26.458 --> 00:05:29.294
-She's just a really sensitive,
tender artist.

00:05:29.294 --> 00:05:31.530
And Nat Wolff was also in our
movie, was in that movie,

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.132
just amazing actor.

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.467
And we have Maya Hawke
in this movie,

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:41.040
and Johnny Knoxville and a bunch
of social-media influencers

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.909
and some pretty wild stuff.
So, yeah.

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:45.744
And we've all been friends
for a while, so it was kind of

00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:48.247
like this experimental family,

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:51.083
kind of fun hang
that we happened to be shooting.

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:53.119
-You play a YouTube vlogger
in the film,

00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:56.422
and there's one scene
where your character

00:05:56.422 --> 00:06:01.827
has to streak down the street
in daytime, in broad daylight,

00:06:01.827 --> 00:06:05.465
and you shot this -- really --
in front of strangers.

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:07.166
What goes through your head?

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:08.835
And here's
what would go through my head.

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:10.903
"Can Andrew Garfield
do this scene for me?

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:12.971
Because I can't."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.971 --> 00:06:14.740
But I mean, do you go,
"I'm just gonna --

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:17.543
I'm an actor.
I'm going for it, and just run"?

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:21.881
-Dude, I don't know, man.
It's like, life's short.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:23.716
If this year
has taught us anything,

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:25.918
it's that life is short,
and if I get an opportunity

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:27.419
to streak down
Hollywood Boulevard

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:29.455
without getting arrested,
I'm gonna take it.

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:31.323
It's --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:31.323 --> 00:06:34.060
It's kind of that simple.
[ Applause ]

